    "text": "**Case A**\n\n- **A1 \u2013 Cannot tell.** The search covered only the past seven days, so earlier misuse outside that window is not ruled out. It is also not established that the burst was misuse rather than the owner acting.\n- **A2 \u2013 Yes.** The proxy, new fingerprint and payout changes happened at 19 minutes, and the check scored the account at 30 minutes without flagging it. This assumes the check could see request and account-change data, which the case does not state.\n- **A3 \u2013 No, not by itself.** A shared payout destination is a link, but it could also reflect a shared processor, an intermediary, or the same fraudster's destination across several accounts.\n- **A4 \u2013 Yes, weakly.** Thirty days is enough time for an owner to notice diverted payouts, so silence is some evidence of legitimacy, but only weak evidence because victims may not have noticed or reported.\n- **A5 \u2013 Yes.** A new network path and fingerprint minutes after publication, combined with payout changes, clearly warrants a look.\n\n**Case B**\n\n- **B1 \u2013 Yes.** Each account has 100 completed transactions, so D's recorded rate is 0 percent against 2 percent for the others. The difference is small and may not be meaningful.\n- **B2 \u2013 No.** A combined figure can hide an individual account above it; C, for example, could be higher once alerts are counted.\n- **B3 \u2013 Yes.** The search covered all merchant accounts and found none beyond A and the closed account. It shows this for accounts recorded under that person; it cannot rule out accounts held through nominees.\n- **B4 \u2013 Cannot tell.** Different analysts on different days does not establish independence; they may have worked from the same model output or the second may have read the first note.\n- **B5 \u2013 No.** A blocked payment cannot generate a chargeback, so the absence of one says nothing about whether it was fraud.\n- **B6 \u2013 Yes.** Shared templates, a common descriptor pattern and